---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Blk from "../components/Blk.astro";
import Note from "../components/Note.astro";
import Scroll from "../components/Scroll.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import c from "../data/chatham.json";
---
<Base
  title="Chatham"
  eyebrow={`${c.parish} · searched ${c.searched}`}
  dek="The Royal Engineers lived at Chatham, and so did this family. Nobody had ever read its register. It holds a general's burial, his wife's, his brother officer's, and a christening eight days before a convict ship sailed.">

  <section class="blk">
    <p class="lede">This archive has said for years that <a href={u("/people/robert-d-arcy/")}>Major-General
    Robert D'Arcy</a> died at Chatham in 1827 — on the authority of <em>Connolly's Roll of Officers
    of the Corps of Royal Engineers</em>, a printed book. It has said his wife
    <a href={u("/people/jean-ward/")}>Jean Ward</a> died there in 1823, on the authority of a family
    tree. Neither statement had a parish record behind it.</p>

    <p class="dek" style="margin-top:1rem">Chatham St Mary the Virgin holds
    <strong>{c.rows.length} D'Arcy entries</strong>. Six of them are this family.</p>
  </section>

  <Blk label="What the register holds" title="Seven entries, in order">

    <div style="margin-top:1.3rem;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:1.3rem">
      {c.rows.map((r) => (
        <div style="border-left:2px solid var(--accent);padding:2px 0 2px 16px">
          <div><span class="mono small" style="color:var(--ink-3)">{r.date}</span>
            <strong style="margin-left:.6rem">{r.who}</strong>
            <span class="chip doc" style="margin-left:.5rem">{r.type}</span></div>
          <p class="small" style="margin-top:.35rem;color:var(--ink-2)">{r.detail}
            <span style="color:var(--ink-3)"> · {r.reg}</span></p>
          <p class="small" style="margin-top:.35rem">{r.means}</p>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </Blk>

  <Blk label="The one that matters most" title="“Robert D'Arcy, 76, Chatham”">

    <p class="lede">The family tree says Robert D'Arcy was born in <strong>1751</strong>. This archive
    has spent months explaining that the 1751 is <em>arithmetic from an age on a burial register</em>
    rather than a birth record, and should not be trusted as a date.
    <a href={u("/hornby/")}>The Hornby claim →</a></p>

    <p style="margin-top:1.1rem"><strong>Here is that burial register.</strong> Entry 1571, 19 May
    1827: <strong>Robert D'Arcy, age 76, of Chatham</strong>. Connolly gives his death as 13 May;
    he was buried six days later, in the garrison town he had served for half a century.</p>

    <Note>
      <strong>And the local paper watched it happen.</strong> The Kent Herald of 24 May 1827: “On
      Saturday Major-General D'Arcy, whose death we announced last week, <strong>was interred at
      Chatham with military honours</strong>.” That Saturday was 19 May. Seventeen papers across
      three countries carried his death, four of them giving 13 May.
      <a href={u("/obituary/")}>All seventeen →</a>
    </Note>

    <Note>
      <strong>The correction stands, and it is now a better-founded correction.</strong> An age at
      burial is still a <em>stated</em> age — supplied by whoever brought the body, rounded, and
      frequently wrong by several years. It is not a birth record and it never becomes one. But the
      archive has gone from repeating somebody's arithmetic to holding the document the arithmetic
      was done on, which is the whole business of this site.
      <span class="chip inf">an age, not a birth</span>
    </Note>

    <p class="dek" style="margin-top:1.2rem">And it does not touch his parentage, which remains
    unknown. A burial names nobody but the dead.</p>
  </Blk>

  <Blk label="Eight days" title="A christening, and then a convict ship">

    <p>The entry that brings the two halves of this archive together is a baptism.</p>

    <Scroll>
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Child</td><td><strong>Charlotte D'Arcy</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Born</td><td class="m">29 April 1825</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Baptised</td><td class="m"><strong>31 March 1826</strong>, Chatham St Mary the Virgin</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Father</strong></td><td><strong>George Pitt D'Arcy</strong> — “<em>Bt: Major 39th Foot</em>”</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Mother</strong></td><td><strong>Maria</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Abode</td><td>Chatham</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </Scroll>

    <Note>
      <strong>On 8 April 1826 — eight days later — the convict ship <em>England</em> embarked a
      detachment of the 39th Foot commanded by “George Pitt D'Arcy, B. Major”</strong>, with thirty-nine
      men, six women, seven children, and Mrs D'Arcy and family in the cabin. They christened the baby,
      and then they sailed for New South Wales.
      <a href={u("/australia/")}>The surgeon's journal →</a>
    </Note>

    <p class="dek" style="margin-top:1.2rem">The rank in the parish register — <em>Brevet Major, 39th
    Foot</em> — is the same rank the ship's surgeon wrote down at sea a fortnight later. Two clerks,
    two countries, one man, and neither of them had ever been set beside the other.
    <span class="chip doc">documented</span></p>
  </Blk>

  <Blk label="And two marriages resolved" title="Mary, then Maria">
    <p>The archive has held that George Pitt D'Arcy married twice — a Miss Ludlam about 1805, and
    Maria White in County Wicklow about 1819 — and that
    <a href={u("/people/frederick-robert-d-arcy/")}>Frederick Robert D'Arcy</a> the surveyor was a
    <em>half</em>-brother to the Australian children. The register bears that out without being asked:</p>

    <Scroll>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th class="m">Baptised</th><th>Child</th><th>Father</th><th>Mother</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="m">2 Jul 1811</td><td>Frederick Robert D'Arcy</td><td>George</td><td><strong>Mary</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><td class="m">31 Mar 1826</td><td>Charlotte D'Arcy</td><td>George Pitt, Bt Major 39th Foot</td><td><strong>Maria</strong></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </Scroll>
    <p class="small" style="margin-top:1rem">Two children of one man, fifteen years apart, by two
    differently named mothers, in the same parish. <span class="chip doc">the half-brother, documented</span></p>
  </Blk>

  <section class="blk" style="border-bottom:0">
    <h2>What Chatham changes</h2>
    <ol class="dek">
      <li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>Three deaths move from books to registers.</strong>
      Robert's, Jean's and Constantine's — each previously carried on a printed roll or a family
      tree, each now in the parish book.</li>
      <li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>Jean Ward has an age at last</strong> — sixty-six in
      March 1823, which puts her birth about 1756 against the tree's 1754. <strong>And her baptism has now been found</strong> — South Leith,
      Midlothian, 19 July 1754, father Joseph Ward, mother Martha Garden. This page said for months
      that South Leith was not transcribed. <a href={u("/jean-ward/")}>It is →</a></li>
      <li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>A daughter's marriage, witnessed by a brother.</strong>
      Margarett Maria Isabella married in 1806 with <strong>Joseph D'Arcy</strong> signing — the same
      Joseph baptised at Portsea in 1780, <a href={u("/hornby/")}>the first record of this family ever
      found in England</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>And Robert D'Arcy's parentage is exactly as unknown as it was this morning.</strong>
      Everything found today names him as a husband, a father and a corpse. Nothing names him as a
      son. <a href={u("/errands/")}>The will that might →</a></li>
    </ol>
  </section>
</Base>
